.TH mbgetphotocorrection 1 "7 September 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbgetphotocorrection\fP \- Generate a 3D table of image intensity correction values from a set of navigated stereo or mono seafloor photographs.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
mbgetphotocorrection [\fB\-\-verbose\fP \fB\-\-help\fP \fB\-\-threads=\fP\fInthreads\fP
\fB\-\-input=\fP\fIimagelist\fP
\fB\-\-output=\fP\fIfile\fP | \fB\-\-correction\-file=\fP\fIfile\fP
\fB\-\-correction\-x\-dimension=\fP\fIvalue\fP \fB\-\-correction\-y\-dimension=\fP\fIvalue\fP
\fB\-\-correction\-z\-dimension=\fP\fIvalue\fP \fB\-\-correction\-z\-minmax=\fP\fIvalue\fP/\fIvalue\fP
\fB\-\-fov\-fudgefactor=\fP\fIfactor\fP \fB\-\-trim=\fP\fItrim_pixels\fP
\fB\-\-reference\-gain=\fP\fIgain\fP \fB\-\-reference\-exposure=\fP\fIexposure\fP
\fB\-\-platform\-file=\fP\fIplatform.plf\fP \fB\-\-camera\-sensor=\fP\fIid\fP
\fB\-\-nav\-sensor=\fP\fIid\fP \fB\-\-sensordepth\-sensor=\fP\fIid\fP
\fB\-\-heading\-sensor=\fP\fIid\fP
\fB\-\-attitude\-sensor=\fP\fIid\fP
\fB\-\-use\-left\-camera\fP | \fB\-\-use\-right\-camera\fP | \fB\-\-use\-both\-cameras\fP
\fB\-\-calibration\-file=\fP\fIstereocalibration.yaml\fP
\fB\-\-navigation\-file=\fP\fIfile\fP \fB\-\-tide\-file=\fP\fIfile\fP
\fB\-\-image\-quality\-file=\fP\fIfile\fP \fB\-\-image\-quality\-threshold=\fP\fIvalue\fP
\fB\-\-image\-quality\-filter\-length=\fP\fIvalue\fP
\fB\-\-topography\-grid=\fP\fIfile\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBMbgetphotocorrection\fP generates a 3D table of image intensity correction
values, indexed by lateral pixel position (x, y with respect to the camera
image) and by standoff distance (z, the range from the camera to the seafloor
along the line of sight). This table is intended for later use by
\fBmbphotomosaic\fP to flatten illumination variation across a photomosaic
built from many overlapping downlooking photographs.
.P
The program reads a sequence of single or stereo pair images and associated
per-image navigation, attitude, and camera settings from an MB-System
imagelist file (in the format written by \fBmbm_makeimagelist\fP or similar
tools). For each image, the camera position and orientation are combined with
an optional platform offset model and an optional bathymetric topography grid
(or, absent a topography grid, an assumed flat seafloor at a nominal standoff)
to project every pixel onto the seafloor and calculate that pixel's standoff
distance. Each image is optionally undistorted using a stereo camera
calibration model before being processed. Pixels are excluded from the
correction table if their line of sight is too vertical (more than 80 degrees
from vertical, since this program assumes a 2D downlooking photomosaic
geometry) or if the standoff falls outside the requested z range. Purely black
pixels, and pixels adjacent to purely black pixels, are also excluded unless
an explicit pixel trim margin is set with \fB\-\-trim\fP.
.P
Prior to binning, each pixel's YCrCb intensity is corrected for the camera's
recorded gain and exposure settings relative to user-specified reference gain
and exposure values, so that images captured under different camera settings
contribute consistently to the same correction table.
.P
The 3D correction table (separate tables are maintained for each of the two
cameras of a stereo pair) accumulates the mean Y, Cr, and Cb values in each
x/y/z bin. After all images have been processed, any empty bins are filled in
by two iterative passes: first averaging over neighboring x/y bins at the same
standoff, then averaging over neighboring standoff bins at the same x/y
location. The completed correction tables are written to an OpenCV YAML file
using \fBcv::FileStorage\fP, together with the correction table dimensions and
bounds and the reference gain and exposure values used. This file can be
supplied to \fBmbphotomosaic\fP with its own \fB\-\-correction\-file\fP option to
correct pixel intensities using this table.
.P
\fBMbgetphotocorrection\fP can process images using multiple threads (see
\fB\-\-threads\fP). Any parameter change statements embedded in the imagelist
file are honored, causing all in-flight threads to complete before the new
parameters take effect.

